"text": "A registry stores images and serves them to anything that needs to pull them: your VM, a Kubernetes cluster, a build pipeline. Docker Hub is the public default. Azure Container Registry (ACR) is a private registry in your subscription. Azure Container Registry Each registry has a login server, <registry name>.azurecr.io. To push to it, you prefix the image name with the login server, sign in, and run docker push. On the lab VM, az login --identity signs the Azure CLI in with the VM's managed identity, then az acr login --name <registry name> exchanges that identity for a registry token that Docker uses. No password is ever typed or stored. The AcrPush role that course up gave the VM is what allows the push. Authenticate with a managed identity The registry of this chapter uses the Basic tier, the smallest one, with 10 GiB of storage included in the daily price. Its admin account, a shared user name and password, stays disabled: every push is then traceable to an identity. ACR service tiers Docker Hub limits how many pulls anonymous users can make in a given time. The lab only pulls a few images, but a classroom behind one public IP address can hit the limit. Docker Hub usage and limits From a Dockerfile to a running container and a pushed image. your push fails with an authentication error, although az acr login succeeded. Which name did you probably push? An image name without the login server prefix, such as sample-app:1.0.0. Docker then tries Docker Hub, where you are not "
