"url": "style-guide.html#labs",
"text": "Every lab has four parts in the kit, in labs/NN-name: a setup script, a validation script and a teardown script, plus the tasks and acceptance criteria on the chapter page. Nothing else reviews the learner's work, so the validation script checks every acceptance criterion that a script can check. The learner runs them through the course command in the environment: course start NN runs setup.sh, course check NN runs validate.sh, course break NN runs break.sh and course clean NN runs teardown.sh. A new lab needs no change to course: it finds the folder by its number. course start NN is the one command a learner runs when a lab begins and every time they come back. It creates what is missing, starts what has stopped, waits until it is ready, and ends with course check NN --next, which says which task is next. It is safe to run any number of times and never throws work away. Each lab that needs a cluster has its own kind cluster, and one cluster runs at a time. lab_cluster_up in lib/lib.sh creates it, records the lab it belongs to, starts its stopped node containers, and connects it to the registry. When the cluster of another lab exists, it asks whether to clean that lab first. The registry on localhost:5001 belongs to the environment, not to a lab. course up creates it with a named volume, course clean NN never removes it, and course destroy removes it with the rest. From chapter 2 on, the nodes pull from it, and no lab uses kind load. The learner's own files live in ~/work/NN-"
