"text": "On Windows, course.ps1 builds and manages the WSL distribution of the course: up, connect, status, check, stop, destroy and update. In the environment, the bash course runs the same commands of the environment and the commands of the labs: start, check NN, break, solution and clean. On Linux the environment is the machine itself, so ./course in the kit folder does both. The two lines ENV_COMMANDS and LAB_COMMANDS of course list the commands. Both checkers read them, and course.ps1 accepts every command of the environment. Every name derives from the name of the kit, KIT_NAME in lib/lib.sh and $KitName in lib/lib.ps1: the WSL distribution, the clone in it, the kind cluster and its context, the registry and its volume. tools/check-kit.py fails when they disagree. Every course command prints the name of its course on its first line. On Windows, course up writes the user data of cloud-init in the .cloud-init folder of the Windows profile, creates the distribution from stock Ubuntu with wsl --install --no-launch, and lets cloud-init create the user learner, run setup/install-tools.sh and setup/wsl-environment.sh, clone the kit and put a launcher on the path. It never edits the learner's .wslconfig. On Linux, course up lists the tools it would install and asks before it runs setup/install-tools.sh with sudo. It then offers a launcher in ~/.local/bin, and names the kit of a launcher that is already there before it replaces it. On macOS it installs nothing. On Linux, connect and stop"
